13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"February 10–July 6, 1862; Battle of New Bern March 14 and Fort Macon bombardment April 25","place":"Elizabeth City, Winton, New Bern, Beaufort, Morehead City, and Fort Macon, North Carolina","result":"United States Army and Navy forces destroyed the remaining Confederate gunboats near Elizabeth City, captured New Bern after breaking the Confederate line at the railroad brickyard, and forced Fort Macon to surrender. New Bern remained a United States base for the rest of the war, but Burnside left with 7,000 men in July before attacking the railroad junction at Goldsboro","facts":[{"label":"Battle of New Bern","value":"March 14, 1862; United States victory after about four hours"},{"label":"Burnside’s force at New Bern","value":"About 11,000 soldiers supported by 13 warships"},{"label":"Branch’s force at New Bern","value":"About 4,500 North Carolina troops"},{"label":"Confederate defensive line","value":"About one mile from Fort Thompson to the railroad, with a gap near the brickyard"},{"label":"New Bern losses stated in episode","value":"U.S.: 90 killed, 380 wounded; Confederate: 64 killed, 101 wounded, 413 missing"},{"label":"Fort Macon","value":"Bombarded April 25; its garrison capitulated April 26"},{"label":"Coastal result","value":"New Bern, Morehead City, Beaufort, and Fort Macon entered United States control"},{"label":"Unfinished objective","value":"The expedition did not advance 60 miles inland to the Goldsboro railroad junction"}],"maps":[{"title":"The North Carolina coast and the campaign objectives","caption":"The episode follows operations from Roanoke Island and Elizabeth City south to New Bern, then southeast to Morehead City, Beaufort, and Fort Macon. Goldsboro, the intended inland objective, lay west of New Bern.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-78.15%2C34.45%2C-75.15%2C36.35&layer=mapnik&marker=35.1085%2C-77.0441"},{"title":"New Bern and the Neuse River today","caption":"Burnside’s troops landed near Slocum Creek, marched north on the west bank of the Neuse, and attacked the Confederate line south of the Trent River and New Bern.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.25%2C34.90%2C-76.82%2C35.25&layer=mapnik&marker=35.0515%2C-77.0161"},{"title":"Troop positions and the break in the Confederate line","caption":"Foster attacked between the railroad and the Neuse River. Reno found the gap at the brickyard and sent the 21st Massachusetts through it. Parke’s reserve followed, struck the 7th North Carolina’s flank, and helped collapse the Confederate left.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=NC003","image":"/images/new-bern-battle-plan.svg","alt":"Simplified map of United States attacks and Confederate positions at the Battle of New Bern"},{"title":"Official route map of the New Bern expedition","caption":"John G. Foster’s official map identifies the March 13 landing, the advance route, the battlefield, river obstructions, defensive works, and New Bern.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447474/","image":"/images/new-bern-route-map.jpg","alt":"Official map showing the route of Burnside’s expedition and the Battle of New Bern"},{"title":"Beaufort Harbor and Fort Macon","caption":"This 1861 map shows Fort Macon on Bogue Banks, the harbor channel, Beaufort, Morehead City, and the position later occupied by Parke’s division.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447458/","image":"/images/fort-macon-1861-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of Beaufort Harbor showing Fort Macon, Bogue Banks, Beaufort, and Morehead City"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:30","audioTime":90,"title":"New Bern was the next objective","detail":"The city was North Carolina’s second largest and the eastern end of a railroad running to the important junction at Goldsboro."},{"time":"3:53","audioTime":233,"title":"United States gunboats attacked Elizabeth City","detail":"On February 10, the ships charged at close range, overwhelmed the Cobbs Point battery, and destroyed the remaining Confederate mosquito fleet."},{"time":"4:55","audioTime":295,"title":"Gunboats moved up the Chowan River","detail":"Confederates attempted to ambush the vessels at Winton. United States troops returned on February 20, landed, ransacked the town, and burned it."},{"time":"6:49","audioTime":409,"title":"Branch prepared the New Bern defenses","detail":"About 4,500 inexperienced North Carolina troops occupied works between the Neuse River and the swamps near Bryce Creek."},{"time":"7:28","audioTime":448,"title":"Fort Thompson anchored the river end","detail":"The fort held thirteen guns, but ten faced the river because the defenders expected the main attack to come by water."},{"time":"8:06","audioTime":486,"title":"The defensive line stopped at the railroad","detail":"Branch shortened the planned works because he lacked enough troops. A gap of about 150 yards remained near the railroad brickyard."},{"time":"8:37","audioTime":517,"title":"Branch assigned the front-line units","detail":"The 37th, 7th, and 35th North Carolina held the river side. The 24th and 26th North Carolina covered the far side, while recently raised militia guarded the brickyard gap."},{"time":"9:12","audioTime":552,"title":"Burnside embarked for New Bern","detail":"The expedition left Roanoke Island on March 11 with about 11,000 soldiers. Commander Stephen C. Rowan led thirteen supporting warships."},{"time":"10:15","audioTime":615,"title":"The army landed without opposition","detail":"The March 13 landing near Slocum Creek succeeded because the defenders expected a river attack. Heavy rain turned the northward march into deep mud."},{"time":"10:45","audioTime":645,"title":"Most artillery and reserve ammunition fell behind","detail":"The infantry stopped about two miles from the defensive line with only a few light howitzers and the forty rounds carried by each soldier."},{"time":"11:20","audioTime":680,"title":"The United States attack began","detail":"Foster advanced between the railroad and river, Reno moved west of the railroad, and Parke followed in reserve."},{"time":"11:46","audioTime":706,"title":"Foster attacked before Reno was ready","detail":"Confederate infantry and the landward guns of Fort Thompson stopped Foster’s advance."},{"time":"12:06","audioTime":726,"title":"Naval shells struck among United States troops","detail":"Some shells from Rowan’s gunboats overshot the Confederate works and landed in the attacking lines. Rowan continued firing until asked to stop."},{"time":"13:14","audioTime":794,"title":"Reno found the brickyard gap","detail":"He personally led four companies of the 21st Massachusetts through the opening and across the railroad against the flank of the militia."},{"time":"13:44","audioTime":824,"title":"The Confederate center began to fail","detail":"The militia withdrew, exposing the 35th North Carolina. Branch committed the 33rd North Carolina reserve, but it could not close the gap."},{"time":"14:42","audioTime":882,"title":"Parke committed the reserve brigade","detail":"Fresh United States troops passed through the opening and hit the right flank of the 7th North Carolina."},{"time":"15:03","audioTime":903,"title":"Foster renewed his attack","detail":"Foster ordered a charge after Parke broke through. The Confederate line between the railroad and the river collapsed."},{"time":"15:15","audioTime":915,"title":"Branch ordered a retreat","detail":"The Confederates withdrew across the Trent River bridge. Two regiments on the right did not receive the order promptly, and many of their men were captured."},{"time":"16:00","audioTime":960,"title":"United States forces entered New Bern","detail":"The retreating Confederates burned the Trent River bridge, but Rowan’s gunboats passed the river obstructions and reached the city."},{"time":"16:49","audioTime":1009,"title":"Both armies reported heavy losses","detail":"The episode gives 470 United States casualties and 578 Confederate casualties, including missing men whom Branch believed were captured or had gone home."},{"time":"19:28","audioTime":1168,"title":"Burnside turned toward Fort Macon","detail":"The fort controlled Beaufort Inlet and protected Beaufort, Morehead City, and the railroad terminus."},{"time":"20:27","audioTime":1227,"title":"Parke began the Fort Macon operation","detail":"His brigade moved by steamer and railroad, repaired a bridge at Havelock, and reached Carolina City on March 22."},{"time":"21:15","audioTime":1275,"title":"Siege batteries opened fire","detail":"On April 25, United States land batteries and gunboats bombarded the fort. Accurate land fire disabled Confederate guns and damaged the masonry."},{"time":"21:45","audioTime":1305,"title":"Fort Macon surrendered","detail":"Most Confederate guns had fallen silent by 4 p.m. A white flag went up at about 4:30, and the formal capitulation followed."},{"time":"22:24","audioTime":1344,"title":"Washington redirected Burnside to Virginia","detail":"Before the planned advance to Goldsboro began, Lincoln ordered Burnside to reinforce McClellan after the Seven Days Battles."},{"time":"23:01","audioTime":1381,"title":"Burnside left North Carolina","detail":"On July 6, Burnside and about 7,000 troops sailed for Virginia. Roughly 8,000 remained to garrison the captured coastal positions."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"Department of North Carolina","subordinates":"About 11,000 troops at New Bern; directed the coastal campaign"},{"commander":"John G. Foster","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"Attacked between the railroad and Neuse River and charged after the center broke"},{"commander":"Jesse L. Reno","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"Operated west of the railroad and forced the brickyard gap with the 21st Massachusetts"},{"commander":"John G. Parke","command":"3rd Brigade","subordinates":"Committed the reserve at New Bern and later conducted the siege of Fort Macon"},{"commander":"Stephen C. Rowan","command":"Naval supporting force","subordinates":"Thirteen warships on the Neuse River; passed the obstructions and reached New Bern"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Lawrence O’B. Branch","command":"District of the Pamlico","subordinates":"About 4,500 North Carolina troops defending New Bern"},{"commander":"William F. Lynch","command":"Confederate naval force","subordinates":"Remaining gunboats destroyed or scuttled near Elizabeth City"},{"commander":"Moses J. White","command":"Fort Macon","subordinates":"Refused Parke’s surrender demand and capitulated after the bombardment"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"Commanded the expedition that captured New Bern and Fort Macon","side":"United States","image":"/images/ambrose-burnside.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666399/"},{"name":"John G. Foster","role":"Commanded the United States right at New Bern and prepared the official campaign map","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jesse L. Reno","role":"Led the troops that entered the gap in the Confederate line at the brickyard","side":"United States"},{"name":"John G. Parke","role":"Used his reserve to widen the New Bern breakthrough and later took Fort Macon","side":"United States"},{"name":"Stephen C. Rowan","role":"Commanded the warships supporting New Bern after Goldsborough returned to Hampton Roads","side":"United States"},{"name":"Lawrence O’B. Branch","role":"Commanded the Confederate defense of New Bern and ordered the retreat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William F. Lynch","role":"Commanded the Confederate gunboats destroyed near Elizabeth City","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Moses J. White","role":"Commanded Fort Macon during Parke’s siege and bombardment","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/battle-of-new-bern-print.jpg","alt":"Hand-colored 1862 lithograph of the Battle of New Bern","caption":"Currier & Ives published this battle scene in 1862. The United States advance is shown against the Confederate earthworks.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/90709073/"},{"image":"/images/new-bern-route-map.jpg","alt":"Official map of Burnside’s route to New Bern","caption":"Foster’s map records the landing, march, battlefield, defensive works, and river obstructions.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447474/"},{"image":"/images/fort-macon-1861-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of Fort Macon and Beaufort Harbor","caption":"Fort Macon guarded the only remaining North Carolina inlet outside United States control.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447458/"},{"image":"/images/ambrose-burnside.jpg","alt":"Portrait of United States Major General Ambrose Burnside","caption":"Burnside’s victories brought promotion, but orders to reinforce McClellan ended the planned advance on Goldsboro.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666399/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 110","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/110-the-burnside-expedition-part-the-second"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of New Bern","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=NC003"},{"label":"National Park Service: Fort Macon","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=nc004"},{"label":"National Park Service: Lawrence O’Brian Branch","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/lawrence-o-brian-branch.htm"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: Navy Civil War chronology","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/research/library/online-reading-room/title-list-alphabetically/n/navy-civil-war-chronology.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Route to New Bern","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447474/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of New Bern lithograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/90709073/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fort Macon and Beaufort Harbor map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447458/"}]}}]}]
